WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.813
Host systems often record evidence that removable media was connected or mounted.

2
00:00:05.813 --> 00:00:16.443
That evidence can be just as important as the contents of the media because it helps link a device to a computer, user session, volume name, or time window.

3
00:00:16.443 --> 00:00:25.744
Host artifacts may show presence, connection history, mount behavior, or access context even when the media itself is unavailable.

4
00:00:25.744 --> 00:00:41.024
On Windows, USB-related artifacts may appear in registry-like data, setup records, event logs, mounted device records, link files, jump lists, shell bags, recent file references, and other system or user artifacts.

5
00:00:41.024 --> 00:00:52.817
At a high level, these records can include device identifiers, volume names, drive letters, first and last connection times, user context, and file interaction clues.

6
00:00:52.817 --> 00:01:08.180
On macOS, removable media activity may appear in system logs, mount history, file system metadata, user activity records, recent items, quarantine-related records, Spotlight-related data, and application traces.

7
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:20.388
On Linux, investigators may review system logs, mount points, desktop environment records, shell history where authorized, device identifiers, and user-level activity.

8
00:01:20.388 --> 00:01:26.865
The platform determines where evidence is likely to appear and how it should be interpreted.

9
00:01:26.865 --> 00:01:31.516
Connection evidence does not automatically prove file transfer.

10
00:01:31.516 --> 00:01:36.914
A system may record that a device was attached, but not that files were copied.

11
00:01:36.914 --> 00:01:39.322
A volume may mount automatically.

12
00:01:39.322 --> 00:01:43.308
A user may browse a directory without transferring data.

13
00:01:43.308 --> 00:01:50.201
A file reference may come from previewing, indexing, opening, copying, or application behavior.

14
00:01:50.201 --> 00:01:52.776
Each artifact needs its own meaning.

15
00:01:52.776 --> 00:01:56.347
Good host artifact review asks focused questions.

16
00:01:56.347 --> 00:01:58.007
Was a device connected?

17
00:01:58.007 --> 00:01:59.834
Which account was active?

18
00:01:59.834 --> 00:02:02.658
What volume or identifier was recorded?

19
00:02:02.658 --> 00:02:05.897
What files or paths appear in recent activity?

20
00:02:05.897 --> 00:02:09.302
Do host timestamps align with media timestamps?

21
00:02:09.302 --> 00:02:16.277
Are there DLP, EDR, operating system, or application records that corroborate transfer or access?

22
00:02:16.277 --> 00:02:22.755
The strongest conclusions come from overlapping evidence, not a single connection record.
